{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Therefore say to the children of Israel: I am HaShem.</b> Meaning: inform them by My name that My attribute is the attribute of mercy, and I will have mercy upon them. And besides this declaration, you shall tell them the order of the good things that I will do for them: <b>and I will bring you out,</b> etc." "Further, He intends by saying <b>therefore</b> the language of an oath — that HaShem swore to perform everything stated in the matter. And so you find that Ezekiel said (Ezekiel 20:6): ״בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא נָשָׂאתִי יָדִי לָהֶם לְהוֹצִיאָם מֵאֶרֶץ מִצְרָיִם״." "And His saying <b>and I will bring you out,</b> etc., is intended to arrange the order of the good things. For first He lightened the yoke of their burden, and that is His saying <b>and I will bring you out from under the burdens of Egypt</b> — which is the intensity of the subjugation; and this was immediately, at the plague of the Nile, when the gangs of taskmasters and officers disbanded, but Israel still worked somewhat, because the dread of the Egyptians was upon them and of themselves they performed light labor. Corresponding to this He said <b>and I will deliver you from their service</b> — that labor would be uprooted from them entirely. And afterward, <b>and I will redeem you</b> — that is their exodus from the land of Egypt, and included in this detail is the splitting of the Sea of Reeds, for otherwise this would be a redemption after which comes destruction, God forbid. Therefore He did not say ״I will bring you out of the land״ but rather ״I will redeem you״ — to denote a bringing-out in which there is redemption, should the enemy pursue in wrath and fury outpoured. And afterward, <b>and I will take you to Me for a people</b> — that is the giving of the Torah, for there we were singled out for Him as a people, and He set His name upon us; and that is His saying <b>and I will be to you for an Elohim.</b>" "<b>Or</b> it may be intended according to what they said, of blessed memory (Berakhot 7a), concerning the verse ״וְנִפְלִינוּ אֲנִי וְעַמְּךָ״ etc. (Exodus 33:16) — for Moses, upon him be peace, asked of HaShem that He not cause His Shekhinah to rest upon the nations of the world, and the Supreme El answered him: ״גַּם אֶת הַדָּבָר״ etc. ״I will do״ — until here. And corresponding to that time He said <b>and I will be to you</b> etc. — and not to the nations of the world, that He not cause His Shekhinah to rest upon them." "<b>Further,</b> He may intend according to what they said, of blessed memory (Exodus Rabbah 6:4), that four redemptions are stated in this verse. And perhaps they are alluded to in the name Havayah that HaShem said at the opening of His words: <b>say to the children of Israel: I am HaShem.</b> Accordingly, the active Name brings about four kinds of redemptions. According to this, the precision of His saying <b>and I will be to you for an Elohim</b> denotes a redemption that comes in the aspect of the final heh, for the name Elohim is called as is known to men of truth. And perhaps to this He intended by saying, after arranging four expressions of redemption: ״וִידַעְתֶּם כִּי אֲנִי HaShem״ — meaning: and this for you is the sign — four redemptions."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :register {:flags {2 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:25:15.876726878Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}